From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: bound a data request whose completion never arrives

What. Every request with data arms a delayed work, data_work, when it is
issued. If the request is still open when it runs -- neither finalized nor
in its busy wait -- the driver logs one dev_err with int_sum, SMHC_RINTSTS,
SMHC_STATUS and SMHC_IDST, marks a data timeout and finalizes the request
through the existing error path: the manual stop where
sunxi_mmc_needs_manual_stop() asks for one, the controller reset when the
stop does not take, the request completed with -ETIMEDOUT for the block
layer to recover and retry. A transfer that takes no stop and still has
SMHC_STATUS FSM_BUSY after finalize gets the same controller reset.
finalize cancels the work; data_seq names the request it was armed for,
and a deadline covers an expiry that was already waiting for host->lock
while the software queue started the next request.

The bound follows sdhci's software data timer (sdhci.c,
sdhci_target_timeout() + sdhci_calc_sw_timeout()): data->timeout_ns plus
data->timeout_clks at the card clock per block, times the block count,
plus twice the bus time of one block, plus 10 ms (MMC_CMD_TRANSFER_TIME),
with a floor of 1 s (SDXC_DATA_SW_TIMEOUT_MIN_MS) for a request that
carries no access time. The per-block access time is the one
mmc_set_data_timeout() derives from the card's CSD, the spec's worst case,
so the bound ends a hang and does not judge a slow card. The busy wait
after a write keeps its own bounds (busy_work, done_work), untouched.

Why. on 2026-09-19 (8 GB board, 6.18.36) a read at an accepted
data-strobe delay left the task in D state for over ten minutes; only a
power cycle freed it. The trigger there was the stop path, fixed since
(mmc-sunxi-stop-the-card-before-tearing-the-data-engine-down): the
same read on 2026-09-25 recovers every failed transfer, 6/6 runs on
HS400ES and plain HS400.
What stays is that nothing bounds a data request the controller never
completes. SMHC_TMOUT cannot (DTO_LMT covers the wait for a start bit),
and the block layer does not: mmc_mq_timed_out() returns
BLK_EH_RESET_TIMER for every host without CQE and for every host with the
software queue -- both configurations of this tree.

What the vendor does. The BSP driver (allwinner-bsp drivers/mmc/sunxi-mmc.c,
sunxi_timerout_handle(), opt-in per controller through the DT property
ctl-spec-caps) arms a fixed SUNXI_TRANS_TIMEOUT of 5 s for every request,
whatever its size. It calls INIT_DELAYED_WORK() in the request path on a
work that may still be queued, and the handler queues itself again every
5 s, idle or not. With nothing latched it masks SMHC_INTMASK and re-runs
its bottom half as a "timeout retry"; with a write still moving it sleeps
in 1 s steps and then gates and ungates the bus clock. A fixed 5 s fails
a transfer the card is allowed to take longer on (an SD write may take
3 s per block, mmc_set_data_timeout()). Here: data requests only, a
bound from the card's own access time, the work initialised once in
probe, and the driver's existing error and recovery path instead of a
second one.

Not taken:
- Generalising done_work: its two cases (a busy clear without DTC/ACD,
  an R1b command without command done) have their own fixed bounds and
  messages, and it only runs on controllers with can_wait_busy.
- A bound on commands without data: the R1b case already has one, and a
  response timeout is the controller's own (RTO).
- Clearing the request with a controller reset in every case: the stop
  path already resets when the stop does not take; a reset on top of a
  stop that worked only costs a reprogram.

Test stimulus: a test-only patch (mmc-sunxi-lose-the-completion-of-a-data-request, not carried)
(test kernels only) masks DTC and ACD for the next N data requests on one
controller.

Falsifier, on the board, with that stimulus, on the eMMC (not the card
that carries root):
  echo 1 > /sys/bus/platform/drivers/sunxi-mmc/4022000.mmc/lose_completion
  dd if=/dev/mmcblk2p4 of=/dev/null bs=64k count=1 iflag=direct
Without this patch: dd stays in D state. With it: after the logged bound,
"cmd 18 RD: no completion after N ms", the stop path's lines, and dd
completes (the block layer's retry reads the data) or fails with EIO. A dd
in D state with this patch refutes it.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
index 10fa85f..b49a564 100644
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -156,6 +156,14 @@
  * done never arrives.
  */
 #define SDXC_BUSY_CLEAR_DONE_MS		1000
+/*
+ * Floor of the software bound on a data request's bus phase. The bound
+ * itself is computed per request from the card's own access time, as sdhci
+ * computes its software data timer; the floor keeps a request that carries no
+ * access time (data->timeout_ns and timeout_clks both 0) from being failed
+ * while it is still being sent.
+ */
+#define SDXC_DATA_SW_TIMEOUT_MIN_MS	1000
 /*
  * Below this the DAT0 poll's first look legitimately beats an interrupt, so a
  * missing R1b busy-clear interrupt cannot be concluded from it. Above it the
@@ -554,6 +562,12 @@ struct sunxi_mmc_host {
 	u32		req_seq;
 	u32		done_seq;
 	struct delayed_work done_work;
+
+	/* bound on a data request whose completion never arrives */
+	u32		data_seq;
+	unsigned int	data_timeout_ms;
+	unsigned long	data_deadline;
+	struct delayed_work data_work;
 };
 
 /*
@@ -1160,6 +1174,7 @@ static irqreturn_t sunxi_mmc_finalize_request(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host)
 	}
 	if (host->cfg->can_wait_busy)
 		cancel_delayed_work(&host->done_work);
+	cancel_delayed_work(&host->data_work);
 
 	host->mrq = NULL;
 	host->int_sum = 0;
@@ -1431,6 +1446,106 @@ static void sunxi_mmc_done_work(struct work_struct *work)
 	sunxi_mmc_complete_deferred(host, mrq, ret);
 }
 
+/*
+ * How long the bus phase of a data request may take: from the command to
+ * DTC/ACD and, on a read, the IDMAC receive interrupt. After the formula of
+ * sdhci's software data timer (sdhci.c, sdhci_target_timeout() and
+ * sdhci_calc_sw_timeout()): the card's access time per block, from
+ * data->timeout_ns and data->timeout_clks as mmc_set_data_timeout() set them,
+ * times the block count, plus twice the time one block takes on the bus, plus
+ * MMC_CMD_TRANSFER_TIME (10 ms). The access time is the spec's worst case per
+ * block, so the bound is generous by construction; it is meant to end a hang,
+ * not to judge a slow card.
+ */
+static unsigned int sunxi_mmc_data_timeout_ms(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host,
+					      struct mmc_data *data)
+{
+	struct mmc_ios *ios = &host->mmc->ios;
+	u64 clock = host->mmc->actual_clock ? : ios->clock;
+	u64 block_ns = data->timeout_ns;
+	u64 total_ns = 10 * NSEC_PER_MSEC;
+
+	if (clock) {
+		if (data->timeout_clks)
+			block_ns += DIV64_U64_ROUND_UP((u64)data->timeout_clks *
+						       NSEC_PER_SEC, clock);
+		/* one block on 1 << bus_width data lines, twice */
+		total_ns += 2 * DIV64_U64_ROUND_UP((u64)data->blksz * 8 *
+						   NSEC_PER_SEC,
+						   clock << ios->bus_width);
+	}
+	total_ns += (u64)data->blocks * block_ns;
+
+	return max_t(u64, DIV_ROUND_UP_ULL(total_ns, NSEC_PER_MSEC),
+		     SDXC_DATA_SW_TIMEOUT_MIN_MS);
+}
+
+/*
+ * A data request whose completion has not arrived within its bound.
+ * The block layer does not end it: with the software queue, and on a host
+ * without CQE, mmc_mq_timed_out() answers BLK_EH_RESET_TIMER, so the task
+ * waiting for it would sit in D state for as long as the controller stays
+ * silent. Fail it here, through the same error path as a data timeout the
+ * controller reports: the manual stop for a transfer that needs one, the
+ * controller reset if the stop does not take, the request completed with
+ * -ETIMEDOUT so the block layer recovers and retries.
+ *
+ * A request that has reached its busy wait is not ours: busy_work bounds that
+ * phase. Nor is one that has been finalized or replaced meanwhile; data_seq
+ * names the request the bound was armed for. The deadline covers the one case
+ * data_seq cannot: an expiry that was already running, waiting for the lock,
+ * while the software queue started the next request from the completion of
+ * the last one and armed data_seq for it.
+ */
+static void sunxi_mmc_data_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct sunxi_mmc_host *host = container_of(to_delayed_work(work),
+						   struct sunxi_mmc_host,
+						   data_work);
+	struct mmc_request *mrq;
+	unsigned long iflags;
+	irqreturn_t ret;
+	bool fsm_busy;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&host->lock, iflags);
+	mrq = host->mrq;
+	if (!mrq || !mrq->data || host->busy_wait ||
+	    host->req_seq != host->data_seq ||
+	    time_before(jiffies, host->data_deadline)) {
+		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&host->lock, iflags);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	dev_err(mmc_dev(host->mmc),
+		"cmd %d %s: no completion after %u ms, int_sum %08x rint %08x status %08x idst %08x%s\n",
+		mrq->cmd->opcode,
+		mrq->data->flags & MMC_DATA_WRITE ? "WR" : "RD",
+		host->data_timeout_ms, host->int_sum,
+		mmc_readl(host, REG_RINTR), mmc_readl(host, REG_STAS),
+		mmc_readl(host, REG_IDST),
+		host->wait_dma ? ", IDMAC not done" : "");
+
+	host->int_sum |= SDXC_DATA_TIMEOUT;
+	ret = sunxi_mmc_finalize_request(host);
+	/*
+	 * A transfer that takes no stop (a single block) has had its data
+	 * engine released by finalize; if the data state machine still runs,
+	 * the next command would wait behind it, the same hang an earlier bug report saw in another
+	 * place. Reset the controller as the failed-stop path does.
+	 */
+	fsm_busy = ret == IRQ_HANDLED &&
+		   (mmc_readl(host, REG_STAS) & SDXC_DATA_FSM_BUSY);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&host->lock, iflags);
+
+	if (fsm_busy) {
+		dev_err(mmc_dev(host->mmc),
+			"data state machine still busy after the timeout, resetting the controller\n");
+		sunxi_mmc_reset_after_failed_stop(host);
+	}
+
+	sunxi_mmc_complete_deferred(host, mrq, ret);
+}
+
 static irqreturn_t sunxi_mmc_irq(int irq, void *dev_id)
 {
 	struct sunxi_mmc_host *host = dev_id;
@@ -2511,6 +2626,16 @@ static void sunxi_mmc_request(struct mmc_host *mmc, struct mmc_request *mrq)
 	host->mrq = mrq;
 	host->req_seq++;
 
+	/* Bound the bus phase of every data request. */
+	if (data) {
+		host->data_seq = host->req_seq;
+		host->data_timeout_ms = sunxi_mmc_data_timeout_ms(host, data);
+		host->data_deadline = jiffies +
+				      msecs_to_jiffies(host->data_timeout_ms);
+		mod_delayed_work(system_long_wq, &host->data_work,
+				 msecs_to_jiffies(host->data_timeout_ms) + 1);
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * Bound the case the UM leaves open: a command whose done bit never
 	 * arrives because the controller is holding it for the busy. The busy
@@ -3204,6 +3329,7 @@ static int sunxi_mmc_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	spin_lock_init(&host->lock);
 	INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&host->busy_work, sunxi_mmc_busy_work);
 	INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&host->done_work, sunxi_mmc_done_work);
+	INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&host->data_work, sunxi_mmc_data_work);
 
 	ret = sunxi_mmc_resource_request(host, pdev);
 	if (ret)
@@ -3414,6 +3540,7 @@ static void sunxi_mmc_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	mmc_remove_host(mmc);
 	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&host->busy_work);
 	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&host->done_work);
+	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&host->data_work);
 	pm_runtime_disable(&pdev->dev);
 	if (!pm_runtime_status_suspended(&pdev->dev)) {
 		disable_irq(host->irq);
